## MVC Pattern — Development, Explanation, and Coding

 ### 1\. Introduction

 **MVC (Model–View–Controller)** is a software architectural pattern used to develop applications by separating the application into three interconnected components:

 1. **Model**
2. **View**
3. **Controller**

 This separation makes the application easier to **develop, test, maintain, and modify**.

---

 ## 2\. Components of MVC

 ### Model

 The **Model** manages the application's data and business logic.

 **Responsibilities:**

 - Stores data.
- Retrieves data.
- Updates data.
- Performs business operations.

 Example:

```
class Student {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    Student(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }

    String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    int getAge() {
        return age;
    }
}
```

---

 ### View

 The **View** is responsible for displaying information to the user.

```
class StudentView {

    void displayStudent(String name, int age) {
        System.out.println("Student Name: " + name);
        System.out.println("Student Age: " + age);
    }
}
```

 The View should mainly focus on **presentation** rather than business logic.

---

 ### Controller

 The **Controller** receives user requests and controls the flow between the Model and View.

```
class StudentController {

    private Student model;
    private StudentView view;

    StudentController(Student model, StudentView view) {
        this.model = model;
        this.view = view;
    }

    void updateView() {
        view.displayStudent(
            model.getName(),
            model.getAge()
        );
    }
}
```

---

 ## 3\. Complete MVC Program

```
// Model
class Student {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    Student(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }

    String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    int getAge() {
        return age;
    }
}

// View
class StudentView {

    void displayStudent(String name, int age) {
        System.out.println("Student Details");
        System.out.println("----------------");
        System.out.println("Name : " + name);
        System.out.println("Age  : " + age);
    }
}

// Controller
class StudentController {

    private Student model;
    private StudentView view;

    StudentController(Student model, StudentView view) {
        this.model = model;
        this.view = view;
    }

    void updateView() {
        view.displayStudent(
            model.getName(),
            model.getAge()
        );
    }
}

// Main class
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Student model = new Student("Arun", 20);

        StudentView view = new StudentView();

        StudentController controller =
            new StudentController(model, view);

        controller.updateView();
    }
}
```

 ### Output

```
Student Details
----------------
Name : Arun
Age  : 20
```

